Z||000018||Rob Durham||08-23-2001||02:07 PM||||Was that with or without the tube oy KY lube??<P>I dont have but about 550.00 and 6 hours in doing my upgrade to the berretta brakes and I bought the Fiero Stores front brake kit for that<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://rs-designs.com/87GT.html" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://rs-designs.com/Banner.jpg">  </A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroburton||08-23-2001||02:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by starfighter007@msn.com:<BR><B>In your profile it says your a chevy lot checker .Does that mean you work at achevy dealership?If so talk to one of the mech.there. but dont let them butcher ripoff artist touch your car.Illbet you just need some brake pads and maybe rear calipers to get your emergency brakes to work.And dont you think you can do this job with our help?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>i did work there but i went to a thrifway for more money, but im getting a new job at a chevy place soon (if i get hired). <BR>but i seen the stuff that thay say it needs replaced and it looks very bad, every thing is rusted and the e brake is froze up and broken . and i can use all the help i can get  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||fieroburton||08-23-2001||02:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B>Is this your primary car?<P>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 08-23-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>yes||||reg||1
Z||000021||ChadMan||08-23-2001||02:41 PM||||Midas is infamous for this sort of crap.  You go in because your brake pads are worn out.  They confirm that you need pads...but wait!  Your rotors are scored, and we can't turn 'em...so gotta replace those too.  Looks like your calipers are old - you need new ones, buddy!  These hoses look cracked (mechanic bends hose at unnatural 180-degree angle)...see?  And by law, we can't let you go out of here without a working parking brake...<P>How do you know you need anything more than pads?  What are your symptoms?  If you see no brake fluid leakage, your pedal is nice and firm and the car doesn't pull to one side or the other, your calipers and hoses are probably fine.  And every Fiero owner knows you can live without an e-brake (I fixed mine more from pride than necessity).<P>I may catch some flack from other Forum members for saying this.  But if money is super-tight for you right now, your rotors don't look too bad and you have a firm brake pedal with no leakage, throw some pads on it and be done with it.||||reg||8
Z||000022||mbramble||08-23-2001||02:49 PM||||Forget the rust and everything that was pointed out to you to try and coerce you into having them do the work.  Aside from the fact that the e-brake doesn't work, how are the brakes otherwise??  Do you feel safe in the car??  Or, a better question is <B>did</B> you feel safe before you went to Midas and got a scare put into you??<P>If so, I would recommend getting a mechanically inclined buddy to help you do one set at a time (both fronts or both rears) so you don't have to lay out for all the parts at one time, if you are short of money.  There are several quotes above that looks like it would cost approxiamtely $250-$300 per set, in parts, <B>IF</B> you need <I>everything</I> for <I>each</I> wheel.<P>The tools required are minimal and no specialty items are needed.  A jack, jack stands (or blocks), the correct sized torx wrench for the caliper bolts (can't recall but someone here will know) and basic handtools.  Folks here can get you all the info you need before you get to work.  It really isn't very hard work and a few hours on Sat afternoon should be plenty of time to do a set of brakes.  When you have the money for the other set, then do them.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||08-23-2001||02:56 PM||||$1700 for a brake-job?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> HOSE-JOB!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>3 ways to get around this:<BR>1) Bite the pillow, spread the cheeks, and don't forget to cry out "Daddy Midas!"<P>2) Take it to a REAL mechanic. <P>3) DIY. The only real *****  about doing breaks is Bleeding. 500 in parts (worst case), a saturday of work, $50 tow to a mechanic, and maybe $100 labour to bleed the system. Total? $650. That's worst case.<BR>My calipers look rusted to shite and back. Rub a wire brush on it, and it's shiny underneath. Hoses are easy to replace. It's not a big deal. You can probably dissassemble the E-brake, scrub, clean, and lubricate, and it'll work fine. Midas is just looking for a new fish to play with. Don't wake up bound and gagged in a wedding dress. Find a REAL mechanic.<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000024||fieroburton||08-23-2001||03:01 PM||||i know a guy who collecs fieros and has put 88 stuff in his 85 but he is always on the run.<BR>and also the brakes feel fine but after seeing the under side its a little creepy but the feel is fin, ill just go get the pads put on and the roters turned because the hoses were looking 50/50 except the right front side wich had some white string or webbing in it popping out from a crack.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||starfighter007@msn.com||08-23-2001||03:10 PM||||Burton what you say? should we all do this sat afternoon or what? I say we change out the front pads first 20 bucks tops piece of cake with our help .You know they are now performing major surgery over the net now a days.surely we can do your brakes here  I think it will be fun. No I know It will be fun to do let us know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by starfighter007@msn.com (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000026||Ken Wittlief||08-23-2001||03:11 PM||||of course there is rust on your brake lines - they are made of steel and steel rusts the first time it gets wet - that doesnt mean they all need to be replaced.<P>  here is a 30 second brake quality test for you - start your car up, let it run for a second, push the brake pedal down as hard as you can, all the way to the floor if possible.<P>  replace any brake lines that burst - If they dont burst now, they wont burst for a long time.<P>  Midas has the golden touch, they touch your car and take all your gold - dont go back there.||||reg||10
Z||000027||fieroburton||08-23-2001||03:16 PM||||i just came back from a test ride ,<BR>i went 40 or somthing in that range and stomped the brakes nothing but sqeeling and smoke, no busting lines .  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (man that was fun)<P>------------------<BR>1984 2M4 SE <BR>         <IMG SRC="http://publish.hometown.aol.com/fieroburton/myhomepage/07_07.jpg?mtbrand=AOL_US">     [URL=http://www.geocities.com/fieroburton/1.html]||||reg||14
Z||000028||fierobaby||08-23-2001||03:27 PM||||burton! seriously had the exact same problem my father did all the work for about $500 so fix it... and so you know do not drive that car!!! you could seriously kill yourself my dad almost did my brake lines split so watch it... avoid driving it till you can fix it all you really need is the Chiltons manual and a diagram from the library :0) good luck need some help please e-mail me popstar02@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000029||Ken Wittlief||08-23-2001||04:04 PM||||no i think he means squealing from the tires skidding to a stop, and the smoke is rubber burning on the pavement.<P> if you can skid your car to a stop your brakes cant be all that bad, certainly not $1700 worth of bad.<P> time to find a mechanic in the area you can trust.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula88||08-23-2001||04:26 PM||||Yeah, the way I've always checked my brake line (1st do a visual, but if they look ok, then) I just get in the car, start the engine and then <FONT SIZE="3"><B>STAND</FONT></B> on the brakes!  I mean try to put your foot through the floorborad!  Do this while sitting still.  You want to hit that peddle harder than you ever would while driving. If they don't leak or burst then, they won't when driving.<P>Buy a Chilton's book.  Brakes aren't hard to so, honest.  Replace the pads, and hell, replace the rotors too.  You're looking at about $200-250 there.  Drive it and see how it does.  You might rebuild your calipers if they need it, but the only reason they'd need replacing is if they have been damaged, have the bleeder screw stuck, etc.  There's really not much to it.  <P>Like someone else mentioned.  For the price of the parts to do everything, a plane ticket, and a couple cases of beer, you could fly a willing Forum member to your house and have your brakes done for less than what they're quoting you!  And still have enough left over to pay the Forum member a couple hundred bucks for his trouble.  ||||reg||6
Z||000031||fieroburton||08-23-2001||04:38 PM||||yea after the test i think the midas guy is full of **** , so im going to just replace the pads and rotors (from the fiero store) but realy how bad can my brakes be , i drove the car from loveland ohio (about 40 miles or more ) to my home in norwood/cincinnati so the brakes cant be that bad. and the hoses arnt leaking .<BR>i did notes that when i press my brakes it clicks  and clicks when i let up (very quet nois) is it normal ?||||reg||7
