Z||000033||sqoach||08-23-2001||04:54 PM||||OMG!!! $1700+!??? You could buy yourself another decent Fiero for that! Go back to Midas, and tell them to shove it. Ok not really. Drive up there in their parking lot and hit the brakes, leaving black marks to show them that your Fiero doesn't need all that sh!t  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>That $1700 is total  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <P>84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||11
Z||000034||Cheever3000||08-23-2001||04:56 PM||||<B>no<P><BR>way</B><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Ken Wittlief||08-23-2001||05:00 PM||||when the brake hoses between the chassis and the caliper get old they will crack if you bend them waaaaaaaaaaaay over - places like Midass LOVE to do this to your brake hoses, then tell you "you shouldnt be driving this car"<P>  what they DONT tell you is the part that is cracked is a protective cover - the part that holds the fluid and the pressure is inside where you cant see it - that part almost NEVER breaks.<P>  but in most states if there are cracks in the outside rubber they make you replace them to pass the state inspection - not a big deal, they are only a few bucks a piece.||||reg||1
Z||000036||AusFiero||08-23-2001||05:48 PM||||What a :BS: quote.<BR>I reconditioned all 4 calipers, the rears with a recall kit which we have to pay for here. New pads all around. Reconditioned the master cylinder and fitted 4 new brake discs. Payed a mechanic to do it and it cost $1100 Australian, or about $690 US. Was going to cost me another $380 AU (US$190 approx) to get the brake hoses replaced. <P>They are definately trying to take you for a ride if I can get all that stuff done here cheaper on a Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||13
Z||000037||fieroburton||08-23-2001||06:09 PM||||how can they tell that my callapers are needing replaced if they did not take them off the roter ?||||reg||1
Z||000038||Mach10||08-23-2001||06:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroburton:<BR><B>how can they tell that my callapers are needing replaced if they did not take them off the roter ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>2 ways:<BR>1) Your callipers are melted, drooping off the mounts, or have other severe visible damage.<BR>2) They are talking out of their --&gt; (_!_)<P>**** midas, don't let them **** you. Go find a better mechanic, and slap them a bill for the hose that they "cracked."<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000039||fierohoho||08-23-2001||06:34 PM||||I would look for your local Fiero club, join if you haven't yet and find a club member who would help you out, you may find someone who has a halfways decent shop and tools who can help walk you through the repair or give you an honest opinion of what your car needs if you feel you need to have a shop do it.<P>There has to be someone near this guy to help, lets get something rolling folks.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000040||rogergarrison||08-23-2001||08:20 PM||||No way. you can get all that done for a third of that. Brakes arent that big a deal, buy all the new parts and find  someone around you know that can do them for you if you cant yourself. You have to mic rotors to knowif there needed. If I dont see damage or leadage on calipers, i dont mess with them. Id do the rest and see if you need a mastercylinder AFTER you done the rest. I was in a hurry when I got my chassis. Had to have front calipers and rotors. That and pads were only around $350. Midas is ripping you royal. Like said above, there one of the worst price gougers in USA, then Sears.||||reg||1
Z||000041||pontiacs4me||08-24-2001||03:00 AM||||Where are you guys getting your prices for parts.   I see people stating 600 for all the parts, I picked up rear calipers for 70 bucks each, and fronts for only 15 each.    I found good rotors from a salvage car, and had them turned, pads are cheap, hoses are like 12 bucks each, were talking 250 max.  I put it all together in like 4 hours.    1700 bucks, I want to own a midas.  Or like 10.||||reg||1
Z||000042||Indiana_resto_guy||08-24-2001||08:42 AM||||Your getting all the good advice on this one!<P>Midas should have done an inspection sheet with measurements for the rotors and pad material left on the pads. Do you have this?<P>Another point about Midas, besides the exopbant prices they charge for labor (X amount of money to install each part) they mark up their parts prices to almost twice the amount you can get them from the parts store.<BR>Find some one in the area to give you a hand and to them a pair at a time and replace the parts you can afford. It sounds like your brakes are working fine now, so for the front you may need, 1 or both rotors, pads, a good glean and lube and MAY need hoses. You can do the rotors, pads and a good clean and lube for less than $200.00. The hose you can replace next time!<P>Fierodave sells an E-Brake kit that has everything from the handle to the rear calipers for less than $80.00! Get a kit! Original GM parts------NEW!||||reg||1
